We're probably using a different metric for "free".
What C does(/did) doesn't count to me as "free".

It's been true since I started using C in 1983 that
a C program does not return memory, once allocated,
to the OS (which owns the memory, not the C program)
until the C program exits, even if the memory is
freed internal to the C program hours before.

If that behavior has changed it is a recent change
unknown to me.

